e said the critics are full of lies and nonsense. Gumbel said the changes will do little to address the basic problems in French education. The plan makes school more interesting, more about what they need to know in the real world and more fun, he said. "And that of course is shocking to the French purists who want you to be cramming things that are useless for your life." He said people think French schools are marvelous places where the students are rewarded for hard work and where great things are learned. The reality is that one out of five kids "are not learning anything at all," he said. <|eot_id|>
