taining social services or a job. McDaniel most likely made the heat list because of a childhood friend with whom he had once been arrested on a marijuana charge. That friend was fatally shot last year in Austin. Interviewed at his Austin home, McDaniel said he was offended at being singled out by West, who is commander of the Austin police district. "I have no background, so why watch me?" said McDaniel. "And if you're watching me, then you can obviously see I'm not doing anything." ## One On The List Was Killed But the list proved prophetic in the case of Jacobi Herring. This month, the 21-year-old was fatally shot in his South Chicago neighborhood. He had just left a late-night party a few blocks from his home when he was gunned down. His uncle, Koland Herring, was not surprised to learn that his nephew was on the heat list. Jacobi, he said, had given in to neighborhood peer pressure. "He wasn't a hard-core gangbanger," Herring, 49, said outside his nephew's home as friends and relatives stopped by to pay their respects. "He was never a shooter. Some of the people that he knew did. They're hard-core. And in order to get around and to be accepted out here, you have to be around them." Cook County court records show Herring was arrested only a handful of times, mostly on suspicion of trespassing and for loitering with known gang members. However, four others who were once arrested with Herring also made the list. In addition, another man who was once arrested with Herring was shot and killed in March in broad daylight. And Herring himself was shot in 2008. "We talked about him getting shot all the time," said Herring's father, Floyd Redmond, 41. "I used to see my son hanging out, and I used to ride down on him. I feared for my son being out on these streets because I know what these streets are all about, man." ## Police Defend List Another Austin resident who made the heat list is Terry McCoy, 20, who's currently in the Cook County Jail awaiting trial for a 2011 a